WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.960
You know Optimus Prime, but do you know Optimus
Primal?

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:06.470
When it comes to the Transformers universe,
there's more than meets the eye.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:13.514
"Before the beginning, there was Primus, and
there was Unicron."

00:00:13.514 --> 00:00:17.789
Now, finding one definitive "Transformers"
timeline is impossible, as Hasbro — the

00:00:17.789 --> 00:00:21.349
company that owns Transformers — has never
declared a "Prime" reality.

00:00:21.349 --> 00:00:24.669
But if you weave in and out of the different
universes that make up the Transformers' incredibly

00:00:24.669 --> 00:00:29.079
complex multiversal tapestry, you'll soon
realize that most sagas are variations of

00:00:29.079 --> 00:00:33.260
the Generation 1 narratives: the original
stories first told in the Transformers' original

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:36.329
1984 cartoon and Marvel comic book series.

00:00:36.329 --> 00:00:40.510
Patterns emerge, commonalities appear, and
from the many we can pull one.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:44.760
Covering it all would take a book (or three),
but we put together a unified timeline — with

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.760
a few deviations to explore the really unique
diverging realities — to help you navigate

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.686
the increasingly complex "Transformers" multiverse.

00:00:51.686 --> 00:00:53.409
So if you're ready…

00:00:53.409 --> 00:00:55.077
"Let's roll!"

00:01:01.871 --> 00:01:06.655
Light and dark, order and chaos, good and
evil. Primus and Unicron.

00:01:06.655 --> 00:01:11.710
First introduced with zero background in 1986's
Transformers: The Movie, the cartoon eventually

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.960
elaborated that Unicron was built by a mad
genius.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.869
That explanation was somewhat underwhelming
for such a momentous character, so the comic

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:21.420
books later assigned Unicron a more fitting
origin.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:25.259
Per Simon Furman’s comics, Unicron is a
dark god locked in an eternal battle with

00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:27.200
the light god, Primus.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.430
The idea soon solidified into an integral
part of Transformer lore, though like any

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:34.270
myth, the details constantly shift.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:39.590
First Primus and Unicron are from a lost pantheon
of gods, then by issue #74, Unicron is the

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:44.560
destroyer of the previous universe, while
Primus had been born to protect the current universe.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.609
The sentient core of the Universe, known as
The One, created Unicron, then split Primus

00:01:48.609 --> 00:01:49.609
off of him.

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:53.810
By the 2013 hardcover history "The Covenant
of Primus," the two gods become a singular

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:57.360
being that develop opposing personalities
and split into two.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.040
Primus and Unicron may wax and wane in importance
through the different realities, but they're

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.729
almost always there, setting the cogs in motion
for billions of years' worth of stories.

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:09.911
In every version of the story, Primus and
Unicron's battle comes to an end, but that

00:02:09.911 --> 00:02:11.640
end is rarely permanent.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.920
Primus creates the planet Cybertron by transforming
into it, and spawns robotic beings in his

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.210
image to do battle against Unicron.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:22.070
Some stories refer to The Thirteen — the
original, legendary Primes who set all of

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:24.050
Transformer history in motion.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:28.500
Other tales, like IDW's Transformers: Lost
Light comics, speak of godlike Transformers

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.530
with Primus' essence split between them.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:34.330
In the movie universe, the first Transformers
are called the Dynasty of Primes.

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:38.500
Regardless of universe, though, the first
Transformers inevitably arise: powerful, wise,

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.670
and ancient, measuring their lives in the
billions of years.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:45.580
Membership shifts depending on the reality,
but counted among their number have been Alpha

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:50.630
Trion, Autobot mentor and possessor of glorious
robot whiskers; Prima, the first Transformer;

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:55.180
and Liege Maximo, the very short-lived big
bad of the Marvel Generation 2 comics.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:03:00.700
And, of course, there's Megatronus, also known
as The Fallen, the very first Decepticon.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:04.700
Time itself is fairly irrelevant to the origin
of the Transformers, as they live for millions

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:08.110
of years: Even the youngest of these beings
are prehistoric.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:11.690
But few of the Transformers portrayed in the
comics and cartoons were among the first born

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:12.690
on Cybertron.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:16.700
Fans of the live action movies might know
the AllSpark as the source of all Transformer

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.660
life, a concept introduced in the first Michael
Bay film.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:24.260
The idea of the AllSpark grew out of the time-bending
Beast Wars cartoons, where the concept of

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:26.840
a Transformer's "spark" was first established.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.710
Like a human soul, the spark is what makes
each Transformer a living being.

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:34.230
When Transformers die, their spark goes out
— they can't just be repaired and turned

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:35.910
back on like machines.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:40.290
Eventually, Primus and the AllSpark were folded
into one origin story, when Transformers Prime

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:43.775
adapted the AllSpark to be the vessel through
which Primus created life.

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:49.620
Early Cybertronian history is murky and chaotic
in even the best-documented of realities.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:53.810
Warring city-states, battling factions, expansion
into the cosmos — there's a lot of ground

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:58.490
to cover, and few pieces of Transformers media
delve into ancient events beyond passing mentions.

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:04.150
There is one aspect of this history that rears
its ugly head time and time again: The Quintessons.

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:08.900
Making a brief yet unsettling debut in the
1986 animated movie, our first glimpse of

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.656
the tentacled creatures was but a taste of
things to come.

00:04:11.656 --> 00:04:14.750
[Laughing]

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:20.299
Quintessons don't always appear at the same
time in Transformer history, but in every

00:04:20.299 --> 00:04:22.550
rendition, they are vicious oppressors.

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:26.770
The Five Faces of Darkness episodes established
that the mechanical, squid-like race had created

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:31.300
and enslaved the Transformers before being
overthrown; In newer interpretations, Quintessons

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.650
aren't creators, but conquerors.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:38.370
In the 2002 Dreamwave comics series, Transformers:
Generation One, the Quintessons once seized

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:40.419
control of ancient Cybertron.

00:04:40.419 --> 00:04:44.490
Their eventual expulsion led to a Golden Age,
one that lasted until Megatron discovered

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:49.599
that the Quintessons still secretly controlled
Cybertron's ruling council, prompting the Great War.

00:04:49.599 --> 00:04:53.740
And in Netflix's War for Cybertron Trilogy,
we learn that before Megatron fought Optimus

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:59.248
and Ultra Magnus, the trio allied under Alpha
Trion to overthrow their Quintesson masters.

00:04:59.742 --> 00:05:03.919
Above any other Transformer, there's always
been one determined to decide his own fate

00:05:03.919 --> 00:05:05.789
at any cost: Megatron.

00:05:05.789 --> 00:05:10.620
"You are either lying, or you're stupid."

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:13.094
"I'm stupid, I'm stupid!"

00:05:13.094 --> 00:05:17.220
Early Megatron was fairly simple, but over
the years, he's transformed from a stereotypical

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:19.620
villain into a character with complex motivations.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:24.450
His story almost always begins the same, rising
to power millions of years ago in the gladiator

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:27.960
pits of Cybertron during a long-past Golden
Era.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:33.169
1986's Transformers UK Annual first established
this path, wherein the brutal gladiator Megatron

00:05:33.169 --> 00:05:35.560
initially aligned himself with Optimus Prime.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:40.389
In IDW's Megatron Origin comic book mini-series,
he's a pacifist who chafes under the corrupt

00:05:40.389 --> 00:05:41.990
Cybertronian Senate.

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:46.210
His dissent drives him to violence: He builds
a devout following through gladiator warfare,

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:49.360
and eventually assembles an army powerful
enough to overthrow the senate.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:53.770
This rebellion succeeds with help from none
other than Orion Pax: who would go on to become

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:55.380
Optimus Prime.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:59.560
But though Megatron's rise often begins with
promise, it always results in a divided, failing

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:01.176
Cybertronian society.

00:06:02.349 --> 00:06:06.610
Across Transformers realities, one constant
almost always remains: the Great War.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:11.189
It begins in nearly every reality, across
every Cybertron, somewhere between four to

00:06:11.189 --> 00:06:15.930
nine million years ago — when Megatron and
his Decepticons declare war on the Autobots.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:19.759
The thing is, rarely since the earliest days
of the cartoon has Megatron begun the war

00:06:19.759 --> 00:06:21.719
simply out of a desire to conquer.

00:06:21.719 --> 00:06:25.949
Rather, the war comes because Megatron feels
that only he has the vision to lead Cybertronians

00:06:25.949 --> 00:06:28.610
to victory, prosperity, and eventual peace.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:32.979
In the Aligned continuity — which is comprised
of the cartoons, novels, comics and games

00:06:32.979 --> 00:06:37.699
starting with the War For Cybertron video
game and ending with 2015's "Robots in Disguise”

00:06:37.699 --> 00:06:42.909
cartoon — Orion Pax and Megatron both fought
to overthrow the planet's caste system.

00:06:42.909 --> 00:06:47.310
But when Orion Pax is chosen over Megatron
to become a Prime, the Decepticon feels seething

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:49.590
jealousy, and sparks the eternal civil war.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:54.759
In IDW's The Transformers: Autocracy mini-series,
Megatron joins with Orion to overthrow the

00:06:54.759 --> 00:06:56.159
tyrannical Zeta Prime.

00:06:56.159 --> 00:07:00.400
But once the formerly peaceful bot gets a
taste of power, he is loath to give it up.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.740
In the most recent account, Megatron kills
Alpha Trion before the War for Cybertron trilogy

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:09.020
begins, because he doesn't really feel his
mentor is leading the Cybertronians down the

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:10.020
right path.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:14.009
The act of murder splits Cybertron into the
Autobots and the Decepticons.

00:07:14.009 --> 00:07:18.759
Evil is a relative term, and Megatron is an
especially complex villain: From his perspective,

00:07:18.759 --> 00:07:22.419
the Great War nearly always begins as a rebellion
against an unfair system.

00:07:22.419 --> 00:07:26.409
Sadly, it usually leads him down a path where
he becomes more brutal and tyrannical than

00:07:26.409 --> 00:07:28.259
those he once fought against.

00:07:28.259 --> 00:07:33.199
The Great War rages for millions of years,
devastating the Transformers' home planet.

00:07:33.199 --> 00:07:37.320
Back in the franchise's earliest days, Cybertron
is so thoroughly decimated that Optimus has

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:42.020
to lead a contingent of Autobots off-world
in search of more Energon — the lifeblood

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:43.949
of the Transformer race.

00:07:43.949 --> 00:07:47.919
Megatron pursues the Autobots, and the ensuing
attack causes both factions' ships to crash

00:07:47.919 --> 00:07:51.039
to Earth, leading to the events of the original
cartoon.

00:07:51.039 --> 00:07:54.749
In some cases, there are planet-wide tragedies
that lead to Prime and Megatron's departure.

00:07:54.749 --> 00:07:59.289
In two of the newest renditions — the War
for Cybertron and Cyberverse cartoons — the

00:07:59.289 --> 00:08:04.370
exodus begins when Optimus ejects the AllSpark
into space to keep it out of Megatron's hands.

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:08.411
With the source of all Cybertronian life missing,
Prime only realizes his mistake as the planet

00:08:08.411 --> 00:08:09.411
begins to die.

00:08:09.411 --> 00:08:13.940
He has no choice but to venture to Earth to
recover the cube, all while the war for Cybertron

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:16.379
pushes the planet past the point of no return

00:08:16.379 --> 00:08:20.930
Meanwhile, in the world of the Transformers:
Prime cartoon, Megatron's lust for power renders

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:22.330
Cybertron uninhabitable.

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:27.259
The Decepticon leader infects the planet with
Dark Energon, a substance not terribly unlike

00:08:27.259 --> 00:08:28.749
our own world's uranium.

00:08:28.749 --> 00:08:32.770
After learning what Megatron had done, Optimus
Prime determines that Cybertron will take

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:38.000
millions of years to recover, leading to the
Great Exodus, with every Autobot fleeing to Earth.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:42.190
While Optimus and Megatron lie dormant on
Earth, life continues on.

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:47.060
In 1996's "Beast Wars," we turn our focus
to the Maximals, the future descendents of

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:48.440
the Autobots.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:52.620
Optimus Primal — yes, that is his real name,
and no, he's not related to Prime — leads

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:56.670
a small group of Maximals through warp space
in pursuit of this story's Megatron.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:01.540
This time around, Megatron doesn't lead the
Decepticons, but their descendents: The Predacons.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:04.300
These warring factions crash land on prehistoric
Earth.

00:09:04.521 --> 00:09:12.867
[Animal Noises]

00:09:12.867 --> 00:09:17.290
Beast Megatron has traveled back in time after
finding a message from the original Megatron,

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:20.080
with instructions to kill the dormant Optimus
Prime.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:23.780
More time travel shenanigans ensue, but the
Maximals ultimately fend off the Predacons

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:25.470
and return to their own time.

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:29.590
Meanwhile, in Netflix's War for Cybertron:
Kingdom, the search for the AllSpark lands

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:33.110
this universe's Optimus Prime and his crew
in the middle of this separate timeline's

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:34.940
version of the Beast Wars conflict.

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:39.200
After what feels like their trillionth crash
landing on Earth, the Autobots and Decepticons

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:43.570
meet the Maximals and Predacons, who have
once again traveled back in time.

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:47.630
The two factions are born from the scant remains
of the surviving Cybertronians, and live under

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:52.820
constant threat from Galvatron and Nemesis:
Unicron's lackeys reborn from future Megatron

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:54.320
and Optimus' dead bodies.

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:58.830
It's never established exactly how far into
the future they come from; The time travel

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:02.940
gets even more wibbly-wobbly in this universe,
but it firmly establishes the prehistoric

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:07.070
Maximal and Predacon battle as an integral
part of the Transformers timeline — and

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:08.560
one that's here to stay.

00:10:08.822 --> 00:10:13.220
Most Transformer tales begin in the modern
era, when the Cybertronians bring their war

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:17.240
to Earth, inevitably entangling humans in
their alien conflict.

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:20.880
The stories of this time are too numerous
and varied to recount, but suffice it to say

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:25.900
you'll be hard-pressed to find a comic, cartoon,
or movie that doesn't drag humans into the Great War.

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:30.180
In the original Generation 1 cartoon and comics,
the Autobots and Decepticons awaken on Earth

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:33.540
in the '80s after lying dormant for millions
of years.

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:38.580
Some more tales, like IDW's 2005 comic series,
show Optimus, Megatron, and their troops landing

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:42.660
on Earth in modern days to begin with, having
never lain dormant at all.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:44.760
Sometimes humans even bond with Transformers.

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:48.510
These human-Cybertronian symbionts are one
form of what's known as a Headmaster, with

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:51.390
the human literally transforming into the
bot's head.

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:55.670
Other times, like in the IDW continuation
Transformers: Regeneration One, the humans

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:58.880
gain superpowers, allowing them to battle
the giant bots.

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:03.310
Whether our planet hosts Energon, the AllSpark,
powerful artifacts, or just good, old-fashioned

00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:07.500
human interference, it seems humanity's home
is fated to intersect with the Transformers'

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:09.178
endless war.

00:11:09.178 --> 00:11:12.090
Unicron was, is, and will be once again.

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:16.640
The massive Planet Eater first threatens the
Transformers in the 1986 animated film, set

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:19.530
in the far-flung "future" of 2006.

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:24.630
Unicron reformats Megatron into Galvatron,
and creates a new, more deadly breed of Decepticon.

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:29.280
Stepping up to fill the shoes of a temporarily
deceased Optimus Prime, Hot Rod becomes Rodimus

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:32.240
Prime and defeats the galactic devourer once
and for all.

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:33.290
Sort of.

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:36.830
After Unicron was defeated in the animated
film, he made a detour through the Marvel

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:39.620
comics continuity, terrorizing their universe
instead.

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:44.050
His first appearance in this series is notable
for setting up Unicron's godlike powers, and

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:47.920
his ability to manipulate the fabric of time,
pulling Galvatron out of the future.

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:53.440
Since then, across these timelines and multiverses,
Unicron's return has become all but inevitable.

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:57.810
In 2018, after over a decade of avoiding the
ultimate evil, IDW comics finally brought

00:11:57.810 --> 00:11:58.810
Unicron back with a vengeance.

00:11:58.810 --> 00:12:03.530
The massive battle unified the Transformers,
and brought 13 years of comic book battle

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:04.920
to an end.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:08.540
Unicron also appears in the live-action movies,
where Transformers: The Last Knight reveals

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:12.800
that Unicron… is the Earth itself, so it
seems we've not seen the last of the Planet

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:13.910
Eater after all.

00:12:14.643 --> 00:12:18.880
Nearly every Transformers story brings the
robots to Earth as they flee Cybertron, but

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:20.710
there is one notable exception.

00:12:20.710 --> 00:12:25.430
2001's Transformers: Robots in Disguise cartoon
is a bit of a wildcard.

00:12:25.430 --> 00:12:29.030
Robots in Disguise does take place on Earth,
but instead of an ancient war spilling over

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:33.470
onto our planet, this particular show sees
Optimus Prime leading his Autobots to prevent

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:37.900
Megatron and his Predacons from locating an
ancient Cybertronian secret: the immensely

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:40.100
powerful Fortress Maximus.

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:43.870
The show never gets into these Transformers'
history much, but there are some interesting

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:45.250
quirks to unpack.

00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:49.300
This version of Ultra Magnus, for instance,
is Optimus Prime's brother, rather than an

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:50.300
old friend.

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:53.870
And on the topic of quirks, who could forget
Megatron's six-changer form?

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:58.390
In this reality, the Predacon can transform
into, among other things, a giant floating

00:12:58.390 --> 00:13:01.000
hand, a bat, and a two-headed dragon.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:04.770
As for the Decepticons, the only ones in the
entire universe are created when Megatron

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:09.600
gets his hands on a small handful of Autobot
protoforms — essentially blank slates — and

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:12.720
brainwashes them into sinister new creations.

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:16.950
Robots in Disguise was the first time a Japanese-produced
Transformers series was adapted for western

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:19.430
audiences, born from the "Car Robots" anime.

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:24.640
This adaptation was also the first taste of
what would become countless official Transformers reboots.

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:28.620
Before this point, each new comic or cartoon
would simply tweak Generation 1's continuity

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:33.820
to fit its plot, but Robots in Disguise cut
the timeline loose and gave creators free reign.

00:13:33.820 --> 00:13:37.997
As a result, Transformers lore has since run
wildly in every imaginable direction.

00:13:41.395 --> 00:13:45.190
"I found my very own Mini-Con!

00:13:45.190 --> 00:13:47.860
Megatron will love me for this!"

00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:51.360
Launched in 2004, Transformers: Armada was
the second full reboot of the Transformers timeline.

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:54.680
In the world of Armada, there aren't just
Autobots and Decepticons — there's also

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:56.940
a third faction called Mini-Cons.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:14:01.420
In this timeline, Unicron disguises himself
as the Cybertronian moon and creates the Mini-Cons

00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:04.470
to kick the Autobot and Decepticon war into
high gear.

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:08.090
The tiny bots have the ability to boost the
power of their larger brethren, naturally

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:12.500
leading to the Autobots and Decepticons struggling
for control over this new breed of Transformers.

00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:16.430
Eventually, both factions realize the Mini-Cons
are too dangerous, and cast them off into

00:14:16.430 --> 00:14:18.080
the far reaches of space.

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:21.970
Predictably, the Mini-Cons end up on Earth,
where they are rediscovered millions of years

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:23.980
later by the Cybertronians.

00:14:23.980 --> 00:14:27.980
This brings the Transformers into the modern
era and, as is tradition, drags humans into

00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:29.370
their fight.

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:33.200
Unicron is defeated by the end of Armada,
but his story continues in two more series:

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:37.840
Transformers: Energon jumps into the then-near
future of 2010 and chronicles yet another

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:39.650
resurrection of the Planet Eater.

00:14:39.650 --> 00:14:43.400
Transformers: Cybertron sees the Transformers
fleeing to Earth, after the black hole left

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:46.260
by Unicron's destruction threatens their home
planet.

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:50.260
Though it's one of the pillars of the "Transformers"
multiverse, this alternate timeline had quite

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:54.100
a few detractors, and the repeated focus on
Unicron left audiences more than a little

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:54.759
burnt out.

00:14:55.472 --> 00:15:00.610
Though 2007's live-action Transformers initially
seemed to be a faithful homage to Generation

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:03.640
1, it would quickly grow into an entirely
new beast.

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:07.490
The Transformers in Michael Bay's films are
not creations of Primus, but of a mysterious

00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:10.680
alien race not-so-cleverly dubbed the Creators.

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:14.330
The later films retconned a lot about the
earlier ones, but the important part is that

00:15:14.330 --> 00:15:17.900
the Transformers come to Earth about 19,000
years before modern times.

00:15:17.900 --> 00:15:22.140
At this point, the Cybertronians plan to harvest
our sun for Energon – until they discover

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:24.710
humans and mostly agree not to wipe us out.

00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:28.840
But the Fallen, one of the original Transformers
from the Dynasty of Primes, wants to take

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:30.080
the sun anyways.

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:33.600
This sparks the division of the Transformers,
and the formation of the Decepticons.

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:37.900
In other words, this is a rare timeline where
the Great War isn't sparked by Megatron, but

00:15:37.900 --> 00:15:38.900
his Master.

00:15:38.900 --> 00:15:41.500
The Fallen is eventually defeated, but the
war rages on.

00:15:41.500 --> 00:15:47.020
A recurring habit of the Bay films is to interweave
Cybertronian history into our own.

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:51.220
In the 2007 film, they're revealed to have
been responsible for humanity's technological advancement.

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:53.850
In Dark of the Moon, they're the reason for
the space race.

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:57.840
In Age of Extinction, the Creators were responsible
for eradicating the dinosaurs.

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:01.620
And according to The Last Knight, they aided
King Arthur, and fought alongside the Allied

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:03.490
forces in both World Wars.

00:16:03.490 --> 00:16:07.900
The Transformers had been on Earth for centuries
by the events of the first film, but the Autobot-Decepticon

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:11.900
war flares up once again when the AllSpark
is rediscovered, kicking off the entire live

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:13.030
action series.

00:16:13.030 --> 00:16:15.790
"It's you and me, Megatron."

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:19.340
"No, it's just me, Prime!"

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:23.680
Then, 2018's Bumblebee once again changed
the live action universe and rebooted the

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:25.270
entire story.

00:16:25.270 --> 00:16:29.060
Instead of Transformers landing on Earth millions
of years ago, Bumblebee becomes the first

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:31.390
Autobot to land on earth in 1987.

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:35.670
He encounters a couple of Decepticons, who
also landed on Earth, as he awaits the arrival

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:39.748
of Optimus Prime and other Autobots as they
flee the war happening on Cybertron.

00:16:40.177 --> 00:16:44.850
While much of Transformers' past is relegated
to hazy, constantly changing myths, the future

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:47.850
remains a diverging branch of possibilities.

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:51.880
In 2008's Transformers: Animated, the Autobots
win the Great War by shooting the AllSpark

00:16:51.880 --> 00:16:54.960
into space and keeping its power out of Megatron's
hands.

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:59.191
Ultra Magnus then leads the Autobots to victory,
after which Cybertron thrives, while Megatron

00:16:59.191 --> 00:17:02.130
and the Decepticons flee to the fringes as
renegades.

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:05.860
This iteration of Optimus Prime is just a
young asteroid miner who stumbles upon the

00:17:05.860 --> 00:17:08.250
AllSpark years later in modern times.

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:12.130
The inevitable clash between his crew and
Megatron sends them crashing to Earth, where

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:14.470
they lie dormant for just 50 years.

00:17:14.470 --> 00:17:18.429
When they awake in a 22nd Century earth, they
find the planet awash in robots thanks to

00:17:18.429 --> 00:17:23.870
a human scientist, who reverse-engineered
Megatron's body and created his own mechanical life.

00:17:23.870 --> 00:17:26.509
Animated is one of the more unique takes on
Transformers history.

00:17:26.509 --> 00:17:32.039
Two Beast Wars characters even make an appearance:
Blackarachnia and Waspinator.

00:17:32.039 --> 00:17:36.370
If it weren't so wildly detached from established
series lore, this next-century continuity

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:40.490
could have been an interesting bridge between
the robotic Transformers of the past and present,

00:17:40.490 --> 00:17:42.519
and the technorganic bots of the future.

00:17:43.150 --> 00:17:48.535
In a franchise as diverging and messy as Transformers,
it's hard to say which ending is the definitive finale.

00:17:48.535 --> 00:17:52.889
But one particular series provides a better
candidate than others: The Beast Wars sequel

00:17:52.889 --> 00:17:54.240
series, Beast Machines.

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:59.330
The Predacon leader enslaves all of Cybertron,
reformatting Maximals and Predacons alike

00:17:59.330 --> 00:18:01.289
into mindless drone servants.

00:18:01.289 --> 00:18:06.059
The Beast Machines cartoon is a dark, dystopian
tale, where a handful of Maximals led by Optimus

00:18:06.059 --> 00:18:09.500
Primal fight a guerilla battle in a war that
was over before it started.

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:12.929
The story introduces a number of fascinating
concepts.

00:18:12.929 --> 00:18:16.789
Transformers are more like cyborgs in Beast
Machines, as they embrace their organic sides

00:18:16.789 --> 00:18:21.230
and become technorganic creatures, ones that
don't so much transform, rather morphing between

00:18:21.230 --> 00:18:22.380
their alternate forms.

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:24.039
"What are we?

00:18:24.039 --> 00:18:25.629
Robots or animals?"

00:18:25.629 --> 00:18:27.759
“Both and neither."

00:18:27.759 --> 00:18:30.700
"Well that sure clears it up."

00:18:30.700 --> 00:18:34.980
This future even has Botanica, what is believed
to be the only plant-based Transformer in

00:18:34.980 --> 00:18:37.070
all the multiversal timeline.

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:40.549
The series ends with the Maximals freeing
Cybertron from Megatron's control, thereby

00:18:40.549 --> 00:18:41.779
saving the planet.

00:18:41.779 --> 00:18:46.379
The entire Transformer race is reborn into
fully technorganic beings, ushering in a new

00:18:46.379 --> 00:18:47.999
Golden Age of Cybertron.

00:18:47.999 --> 00:18:51.821
Outside of the Beast Machines cartoon, this
future was only ever briefly explored in a

00:18:51.821 --> 00:18:53.690
couple of short-lived comic books.

00:18:53.690 --> 00:18:57.909
So while this technorganic Cybertronian destiny
isn't quite a universal ending, it provides

00:18:57.909 --> 00:19:01.729
the closest thing to a final, happy ending
the Transformers franchise has ever received.

